#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5db6aba9-6cbd-5512-a3ea-6f40db46a0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-3t}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370088} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc763d3-259f-5b21-8237-15af711651e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370089} {:answer/id #uuid "36e87f42-d4ef-52b0-8e1e-8d04ef9a352e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370090} {:answer/id #uuid "2a97d9b6-6569-5dc8-a7a6-9ed5332f6d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(1-{e}^{-3t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370091} {:answer/id #uuid "10a0052f-0e56-5c86-96e4-acfa2390fd1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(1-{e}^{-t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a97d9b6-6569-5dc8-a7a6-9ed5332f6d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(1-{e}^{-3t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370091} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb64710-408a-53fa-9bef-4e2f33202084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edb23ffa-161e-5239-9f82-d504d4a4ac87" :question/math-academy-id "q-330850" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s+3)},\\,s>-3\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-(-3)}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{e}^{-3t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
